# Copy to `.env` and adjust. `.env` is git-ignored.

# development | test | production
NODE_ENV=development

# Port the API listens on.
PORT=4000

# Origin allowed by CORS. Must match the Vite dev server.
CLIENT_ORIGIN=http://localhost:5173

# Per-visitor demo sandbox. When true, POST /api/auth/demo gives each visitor a
# private, temporary copy of the seeded demo account (demo@khzenty.test, made by
# `npm run db:seed`) and signs them into it. Copies cannot upload photos and are
# deleted after 24 hours. Off by default; when off the route returns 404.
# Enable only on a demo or portfolio deployment.
DEMO_LOGIN_ENABLED=false

# Most demo copies alive at once. Beyond this, demo sign-in answers 503 until
# older copies expire. Bounds how much a flood of visitors can write.
DEMO_MAX_USERS=200

# Reverse-proxy hops to trust when reading the client IP (rate limiting, logs).
# 0 = none (default, correct when the API is exposed directly). Behind a single
# load balancer set 1. Too high lets clients spoof their IP; too low rate-limits
# the proxy itself instead of the client.
TRUST_PROXY=0

# Direct (non-pooled) connection string, used ONLY by `prisma migrate`. Leave
# unset locally: migrations then use DATABASE_URL. On Neon, set DATABASE_URL to
# the pooled ("-pooler") address and this to the direct one, because migrations
# take an advisory lock that PgBouncer's transaction pooling cannot provide.
# The running server never reads this.
# DIRECT_URL=postgresql://user:password@ep-xxxx.region.aws.neon.tech/neondb?sslmode=require

# PostgreSQL connection string. Required — the server refuses to boot without it.
# Prisma reads this too, via prisma.config.ts.
# Port 5433 matches the host mapping in compose.yaml ("5433:5432").
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://postgres:postgres@localhost:5433/khzenty?schema=public

# Secret used to sign session tokens. Required, minimum 32 characters, and
# deliberately without a default so a shared fallback can never reach
# production. Generate a fresh one per environment:
#   node -e "console.log(require('node:crypto').randomBytes(48).toString('base64url'))"
JWT_SECRET=replace-me-with-a-generated-value-of-at-least-32-chars

# Session lifetime, as an ms-compatible duration string.
JWT_EXPIRES_IN=7d

# Name of the httpOnly cookie carrying the session token.
AUTH_COOKIE_NAME=khzenty_session

# --- Cloudinary (image upload) -------------------------------------------
# All three are OPTIONAL: the server boots without them and the upload
# endpoints return 503 until they are set. Copy the values from your
# Cloudinary dashboard (Settings -> API Keys).
#
# CLOUDINARY_API_SECRET is a real secret. It signs upload requests and must
# never reach the browser or be committed.
CLOUDINARY_CLOUD_NAME=your-cloud-name
CLOUDINARY_API_KEY=your-api-key
CLOUDINARY_API_SECRET=your-api-secret

# Root folder for uploads. Each user is confined to <root>/users/<userId>.
CLOUDINARY_FOLDER=khzenty

# Maximum accepted image size in bytes (default 10 MiB). Enforced after
# upload by reading the asset's real byte count — Cloudinary's upload API has
# no max-size parameter that can be signed.
CLOUDINARY_MAX_BYTES=10485760

# Formats signed into the upload request, so the client cannot widen them.
CLOUDINARY_ALLOWED_FORMATS=jpg,jpeg,png,webp,avif

# --- Seeding a non-local database (production demo) ------------------------
# `npm run db:seed` refuses any database that is not localhost unless
#   SEED_ALLOW_PRODUCTION=true
# is set for that one command (never put it in a deployed environment). In that
# mode it refreshes ONLY the demo template account. Optionally:
#   SEED_PHOTOS_FILE=default   # attach photo links from prisma/demo-photos.json
# (create it with `npm run db:export-demo-photos` against the dev database).
#   SEED_DEMO_FILE=default     # build the template from prisma/demo-export.json
# (create it with `npm run db:export-demo` against the dev database) instead of
# the built-in sample data. Dates in it are relative to the moment of seeding.
